## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical zero-trust for admin access, mirroring real enterprise risk of credential theft and lateral movement in hybrid environments.

## Key Concepts
- Zero-trust, device posture, MFA, PIM, Just-In-Time
- Jump hosts, session recording, RBAC, break-glass, SIEM/audit

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-policy: grant only if posture true and MFA passed
if (device.posture && user.mfa && time.inWindow) {
  grantAccess();
} else {
  denyAccess();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure effectiveness (MTTR, number of break-glass events)?
- How would this adapt to cloud-native IAM vs PAM solutions across AWS/Azure?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a CISSP-aligned security program for a high‑risk, multi‑cloud data path handling sensitive payments data, with practical controls and tooling.

## Key Concepts
- STRIDE/PASTA threat modeling across data flows
- SBOMs and software supply chain controls
- Reproducible builds and artifact signing
- Per-event attestation and runtime integrity checks
- Centralized key management (KMS/HSM) and ABAC/LNT access controls
- IR playbooks and validation tooling

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: sign payload in CI/CD using HSM and verify at runtime
function signPayload(payload, keyHandle) {
  return HSM.sign(payload, keyHandle);
}
function verifyPayload(payload, sig, publicKey) {
  return crypto.verify(payload, sig, publicKey);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would attestation be validated across multiple cloud providers?
- How would you simulate a breach in a tabletop exercise and measure response time?

## Why This Is Asked
This checks ability to design secure, compliant AI deployments across clouds, covering governance, identity, data integrity, and responder readiness.

## Key Concepts
- CISSP domains: Security & Risk Management, IAM, Security Architecture, Security Operations
- Policy-as-Code (OPA/Rego)
- Model provenance, artifact signing, immutable logs
- ABAC, least privilege, cross-cloud auditable logs
- Incident response for model poisoning and supply-chain risk

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: sign artifact (simplified)
const crypto = require('crypto');
function signArtifact(data, privKey) {
  const sign = crypto.createSign('SHA256');
  sign.update(data);
  sign.end();
  return sign.sign(privKey, 'hex');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you integrate policy checks into CI/CD and runtime gates?
- How would you validate provenance and detect poisoned models before serving?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests practical threat modeling and cross-cloud IAM discipline for edge and cloud architectures—a core CISSP competency for managing distributed fleets and real-time collaboration platforms.

## Key Concepts

- Zero Trust and IAM across edge and cloud environments
- STRIDE/PASTA threat modeling methodologies
- Ephemeral credentials and short-lived tokens (5-15 minute validity)
- On-device attestation using TPM/TEE technologies
- SPIFFE/SPIRE identities and ABAC with OPA policy enforcement
- Centralized KMS/HSM key management with automated rotation
- Incident response tabletop exercises and automation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo token generation outline
async function generateShortLivedToken(identity, scopes, ttlSeconds = 900) {
  const now = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000);
  const payload = {
    sub: identity,
    iat: now,
    exp: now + ttlSeconds,
    scope: scopes.join(' '),
    iss: 'video-platform-auth'
  };

  const token = await jwt.sign(payload, await getSigningKey());
  return { token, expires_in: ttlSeconds };
}

async function getSigningKey() {
  const keyId = await rotationService.getCurrentKeyId();
  return rotationService.getKey(keyId);
}
```
